
`native/` holds the AUnit tests for `InterfaceMotorcontroller`
(`../lib/MotorcontrollerCore/src/`), run natively (no microcontroller attached).

- `native/tests/` -- `test_InterfaceMotorcontroller.cpp` only covers the roboteq-style
  serial protocol layer (term splitting, command dispatch). PID/config/safety behavior
  is exercised by `Shared Firmware Libs/SteeringActuator`'s own test suite instead, not
  duplicated here -- see that library's own `test/README`.
- `native/support/` -- a small, self-contained set of Arduino-shaped stubs, not a real
  EpoxyDuino dependency (see any Triton module's own `test/README` for "why no
  EpoxyDuino"). Notably has a `NativeSerial` with an **injectable RX queue and
  captured TX buffer** (`Serial.inject("...")` / `Serial.sent()` / `Serial.clearSent()`)
  -- this is the first Triton module whose native tests parse live serial input, unlike
  every sibling module's LCD/button-driven interfaces. Also has a `FlashStorage.h` fake
  (SAMD-only in reality; holds values in memory for the test process) and a `pwm()`
  stub (this board's real PWM API -- see `SteeringActuator.cpp`'s `writePwm()`).
- `native/MotorcontrollerNativeTests.cpp` -- the single AUnit driver.
- `native/run_tests_msvc.ps1` -- MSVC fallback for Windows without GCC.

```
pio run -e native
.pio/build/native/program(.exe)
```

## Known gaps

- **This board's pin budget is a real, hardware-forced compromise, not an oversight.**
  The Seeedstudio XIAO SAMD21 target has 11 GPIO pins total; `SteeringActuator::Pins`
  has 18 distinct roles (matching Salacia's much larger Teensy boards). Confirmed with
  the user: single-motor-only (`Config::Electric1`, fixed -- motor2/pressure sensing is
  never functionally exercised), and all four safety interlocks
  (e-stop/operator-present/steer-limit-left/-right) share one physical pin -- a real
  loss of independent-interlock granularity versus Salacia's boards. See
  `ConfigMotorcontroller.hpp`'s own comment for the full pin-budget reasoning.
- `overpressureThreshold`/`overcurrentThreshold` are hardcoded to 255 (effectively
  disabled) in `InterfaceMotorcontroller::ApplySettingsToActuator()` -- this board has
  no real current/pressure sensing wired (see the pin-budget note above), so leaving
  the check enabled against floating/noise ADC readings would just be a nuisance-fault
  source, not real protection.
- `errorFraction` (roboteq `!VAR 7`) is a vestigial, flash-persisted value with **no
  functional effect** -- it belonged to the legacy sketch's own encoder-vs-PWM-delta
  watchdog math, which no longer exists (replaced entirely by `SteeringActuator`'s own
  overcurrent/overpressure/interlock safety system). Kept only so the roboteq wire
  protocol and the legacy Python tuner GUI's variable set don't shrink.
- The real `seeed_xiao` env has not been build-verified on real hardware -- only via
  `pio run -e seeed_xiao`'s cross-compile in this environment.
- Encoder2/motor2/current/pressure pins are wired to genuinely idle or shared pins with
  no real sensor/actuator behind them (see `ConfigMotorcontroller.hpp`) -- their
  readings are meaningless noise, not a bug, given the single-motor-only scope above.

More information about PlatformIO Unit Testing (not used here -- pio test's own
Unity-based runner doesn't understand this project's AUnit setup):
- https://docs.platformio.org/en/latest/advanced/unit-testing/index.html
